Implement secure login and role-based access control.

The system must support three main roles:

## 3.1 Division Manager

The manager should have full administrative privileges, including:

- View all division information.
- Create, edit, delete, and archive authorized records.
- Manage employee records.
- Approve and reject leave requests.
- Approve administrative requests.
- Manage reports and correspondence.
- Manage equipment and inventory.
- Review maintenance schedules.
- Manage application users and permissions.
- View audit logs.
- Generate and export reports.
- Configure system settings.
- Perform backup and restoration.

## 3.2 Engineer

Engineers should have permissions appropriate to their responsibilities:

- View authorized employee information.
- Create and update permitted administrative records.
- Upload reports and documents.
- Register equipment problems.
- Submit maintenance requests.
- Submit leave requests.
- Create internal tasks and follow-ups.
- View approved reports and internal documents.
- Track assigned tasks and requests.

Engineers must not access restricted administrative settings or perform manager-only approvals.

## 3.3 Viewer

Viewers should have read-only access to explicitly authorized information.

They must not:
- Modify records.
- Delete information.
- Approve requests.
- Manage users.
- Change system settings.
- Upload or edit restricted documents.

Permissions must be enforced on the backend, not merely by hiding buttons in the interface.

Do not allow public registration.

Provide a secure initial administrator setup process. Never use a hard-coded universal password. Require the initial administrator to create a secure password and change it when appropriate.

Store passwords using a modern password-hashing algorithm such as Argon2id.

